The study, led by the Chinese Academy of Sciences, involved more than 60 researchers in Tibet, the world's third-largest ice reservoir. The plateau is considered as important as the North Pole and the South Pole in terms of its role in global climate control and global climate change forecasting.
But the latest findings, compiled by these researchers, show that glaciers, lakes and rivers in the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau are changing dramatically.
"There are three imbalances," said Xu Paqing, a researcher at the Qinghai-Tibet Institute of the Chinese Academy of Sciences. The first is the imbalance between the rate of ice and liquid water, with glaciers and slopes having less ice and rivers and lakes having more liquid water. The amount of melting ice has increased.
The second point is the imbalance in the surface of the ice because the inside of the ice is getting hotter. The third point is that the plateau distributes water to different areas unequally, as it sends more water to the east and north than to the west and south.
Over the past 50 years, glaciers and glaciers have begun to melt faster, leading to higher river levels. For more than 60 years, the earth has been warming at an unprecedented rate. This situation resulted in 15% smaller glaciers and caused several large avalanches.
