(China): Chinese companies that make solar panels are looking to expand their markets after the European Union recently announced the abolition of anti-dumping measures and measures. Against the backed goods imported with the support of the countries that export to China's solar panels imported to the European Union.
Chinese solar power products sold in the EU will no longer need to comply with the EU minimum wage restriction (MIP). This is due to the lifting of the ban on imports of Chinese solar panels, which was implemented five years ago. This is good news for many Chinese companies that make materials for solar power.
"The EU decision really helped us in a timely manner," said Wang Lichung, deputy general manager of Hunan Red Solar Science and Technology Co., Ltd. It gives us a new market for the products we produce.
But due to the strict measures against the sale of imported goods that are lower than the market price and the anti-import measures supported by the exporting countries, it is very difficult for those major manufacturers to do business. But now that the restrictions have been lifted, we can work with those manufacturers.
"And we will also find it easier to sell our products to the European market. "EU restrictions include setting prices and a minimum amount of solar goods that the EU allows to be imported from China."
Prior to the restrictions, the EU was China's largest consumer of solar products, accounting for 70 percent of China's total solar exports in 2011. Customers in Turkey, France, Germany and the United Kingdom have all expressed interest in buying Chinese solar products, which are likely to start early next year.
While the Chinese Ministry of Commerce said it hoped China's solar trade with the EU would return to normal, one analyst said it was not yet possible to predict how much trade would return. As before, to what extent. This is further confirmed by Mr. Suli.
According to IHS Markit, a senior analyst at IHS Markit, under the minimum pricing policy for these imported goods, many Chinese companies that make solar equipment use factories in Southeast Asia to produce their products and then export them to the Union. Europe.
