(Isupi): The Ishupi-Digibuti railway, funded by China, has boosted Ethiopia's economic growth and regional trade. This is according to the Secretary General of the Ethiopian Chamber of Commerce.
The 756-kilometer Ishupi-Digibuti Railway is the first modern electric railway in East Africa. It was built with a total investment of 4 billion US dollars from the Chinese government. The railway line in Ethiopia cost $ 3.4 billion. Of that, 70 percent came from the Exim Bank of China and another 30 percent from the Ethiopian government.
The railway provides both passenger and freight services from the Ethiopian capital of Addis Ababa to Djibouti. The railway plays an important role in aiding the flow of business, as Ethiopia relies heavily on Djibouti ports to export and import more than 90% of its goods. Prior to the railroad, transport took three days. But now it has less than a day left.
In addition to providing transportation to the government, the Ethiopian government also hopes that the railway project will boost the nation's manufacturing sector, and the government plans to establish 14 new economic development zones along the railway.
"The connection between the capital of Addis Ababa and Djibouti via rail is a strong point or an opportunity that these economic development zones should take advantage of," said Andal Kacushim, secretary general of the Ethiopian Chamber of Commerce. Thus, the entire economic flow, as we see it today, is directly or indirectly related to the efficient transportation systems needed to cross-border trade.
Combined with other government measures, such as devaluing the national currency in 2017, many economists say the railway project will spur development.
"The investment being made for infrastructure development is important for attracting large-scale investment that creates decent jobs for people, especially young people," said James Wagaga, UNDP Economic Adviser in Ethiopia. Graduate or graduate from various technical institutes that investors need to choose to come to work.
Ethiopia also hopes the railway project will help the country benefit from technology transfer. Chinese experts are training Ethiopian personnel on various operational skills to help them transfer the management of the railway smoothly into the future. (China's CCTV)
Wage Discussion: Each Party Will Take Their Official Official Figures for Tripartite Discussion Again on September 14
(Phnom Penh): Regarding the process of negotiating the minimum wage in 2019 for workers in the textile, garment and footwear sector, which took place in a better environment, Mr. Heng Sour, Deputy Secretary of the Secretariat of the Labor Advisory Committee, stated that each party will Take the official figures from the internal discussions to the tripartite discussion on September 14, 2018 soon.
This statement by Mr. Heng Sour was made after the meeting of the tripartite technical working group to discuss the minimum wage in the textile, garment and footwear sector for 2019 on September 10, 2018 at the Ministry of Labor and Training. Bio.
He said the meeting was the beginning of a detailed and comprehensive technical discussion that was acceptable to all parties to provide a basis for the Labor Advisory Committee to decide.
This mechanism has been implemented since 2014 so that the employers 'representatives, the workers' representatives (unions) and the government representatives can present their position based on the criteria for determining the agreed minimum wage. Come and be determined by law.
Regarding productivity, Mr. Heng Sour said that it is a joint work between employers and workers, which means that employers have brought new and modern machines, and workers have used machines more efficiently, the cost of production. Higher output increases the value added of domestically produced goods over the previous year, and when divided by the number of workers working in the sector, the productivity of a worker in value-added has increased.
He said that on average over the past 10 years, productivity growth in the textile, garment and footwear sector in Cambodia has been 1.6%.
He stressed that the closure of factories does not have a negative impact on the employment sector in Cambodia, although some factories have been closed, but there are a series of new factories, and factories that do not close are expanding. More production, therefore, makes the demand for workers remain the same every year.
