Minister of Agriculture urges AFD to continue supporting Cambodia in economic development to become a high middle income country
The Minister expressed his deep gratitude to AFD for its full support in the development process of Cambodia, especially in supporting projects related to the agricultural sector over the past 20 years.
Through Ms. Florence, Minister Veng Sakhon hopes and believes that AFD will continue to support the Royal Government of Cambodia in further developing Cambodia, which is an important driver in driving the national economic growth to reach Achieving the vision of the Royal Government in 2030 is to turn Cambodia into a high middle income country.
She praised Cambodia's current progress under the leadership of the Royal Government and congratulated Minister Veng Sakhon on his appointment as Minister for the sixth term.
The Minister praised the results of AFD's project, which was achieved with pride. To increase the efficiency of current and future project implementation, the Minister made the following recommendations:
1: To improve the management and use of water efficiently and targeted to reduce farmers' costs, increase yields and increase incomes.
2. Promote the formulation of policies related to the development and management of agriculture and water from the national level to the community in accordance with the growth trends of the national economy and population growth.
3. Request AFD to consider developing a mixed agriculture project that does not focus on rice only and should include raising livestock, vegetables and potential crops on the market.
The talks were concluded in an atmosphere of friendship and good cooperation based on the common principle of developing Cambodia's agricultural sector in the near future.
According to police in Harbin, the fraud cost the state about $ 14 million in tax revenue. The case was discovered by the city's tax department during a regular tax review in October 2015.
After a preliminary investigation, police found that a jewelry company had modified some VAT invoices to avoid paying taxes. And in fact, the address of the company is the address of a hotel that no employee in the hotel knows the name of the company.
During the investigation, police relied on technology experts available in China to identify the suspects.
"After we posted the suspect's information on the Internet, we found that his identity card was rarely used," said Pao Yongjing, a member of the Hebei Economic Crimes Investigation Team. He did not take the train or stay in any hotel. In fact, he is in hiding.
A police scan revealed that the suspect's daughter, Wang, was studying at a university in eastern China's Zhejiang Province.
"Her daughter's bank card and phone card were not registered using her personal information," said Wang Zhicheng, a police officer with the Hebei Economic Crimes Investigation Team. "Use someone else's account name or an account name that can not be verified."
Police later discovered that the suspect's wife frequently sent money to her daughter, which led to the discovery of a cafe owned by the suspect's wife, located in Nanjing, Jiangsu Province, eastern China. Police later arrested the suspect in July 2018.
Police have not yet been able to determine the extent of the suspect's crime and how he deceived tax officials.
In October, China will introduce a new law on personal income tax, raising the monthly tax exemption level from about $ 512 to about $ 732.
The new law also includes tax deductions for special expenses such as the payment of care for the elderly, school fees for children and medical expenses.
