The 4th Svay Rieng Cup has 52 public football teams participating
(Svay Rieng): The 4th Svay Rieng Cup 2018 was held on September 9, 2018 with the participation of 52 public football teams from 8 districts throughout Svay Rieng province.
This is the fourth year of the event, with only 35 teams participating last season, but this year up to 52 teams and a total of 1,500 officials and players.
The Svay Rieng Cup was established in 2013 with the initiative of Mr. Dy Vichea, Vice President of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia and President of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia in Svay Rieng.
The competition is scheduled to start from September 16, 2018 onwards, with no opening ceremony until January 7, 2019, only the closing ceremony.
In terms of participation, all sports teams can have players from 16 years old, with a maximum of 23 people and a minimum of 17 people, with 3 people can bring players from other provinces besides Svay Rieng and 20 others. You live in Svay Rieng or have your place of birth in Svay Rieng.
The formula of the competition is divided into 13 groups, with 4 teams in each group, and a round robin competition in the group to take the first and second place teams to the next 32 teams. Separately, the third best team in the group of 6 teams also have a chance to advance to the next round. For the 32nd round, 16 teams, 8 teams, the last 4 teams will be drawn to meet each other.
Ministry of Water Resources: 17 canals with a total length of more than 42 km have been rehabilitated to save rice people affected by drought in Prey Veng province
(Prey Veng): About 15,000 hectares of arable land in Ba Phnom, Mesang, Svay Antor and Kamchay Mear districts have been intervened by the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology. This is according to Mr. Chan Yutha, spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, told Fresh News this morning, September 10, 2018.
"The Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology has been conducting interventions to bring water to save rice for more than a month, starting from August 10, with the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology using a total of nine excavators," he said. "The canal brings water from the Mewaiko canal to the fields of the farmers who are at risk."
