Understand the law: common property and separate property of the couple
Therefore, the legal awareness program of Fresh News in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation would like to raise the topic of "common property and separate property of the couple" to share to the people, students: What assets? Must be considered as common property and which assets should be considered as separate assets?
1. Separate assets Separate assets are assets that belong to one of the following spouses (spouses):
A. The property that the spouse had before the marriage means that the spouse had the property before the marriage, even after marriage, which is considered to be the separate property of the spouse.
Example: Husband has an apartment before marriage, even after marriage, that house is still the property of the husband, can not be counted as joint property.
B. Property acquired by a spouse by inheritance or inheritance during marriage. In case the owner gives the property to the spouse, it means that the gift will become the separate property of the spouse. Unless the donor states that both the husband and the wife become joint property of the couple.
C. Assets are the value associated with the disposition of a separate asset, which is considered to be a separate asset.
2. Common property Common property is any property acquired by both spouses or during the marriage in the form of salary or wages or wages or benefits other than the above three points.
Example: Husband works as a civil servant and earns 1 million Riel per month during marriage. Therefore, the husband's salary is considered to be the common property of the husband and wife. In case the husband earns only one income and the wife is only a housewife doing housework (domestic work), is the income earned only by the husband considered as joint property or separate property?
As in the example above, the husband works as a civil servant and receives a salary of 1 million riel per month during the marriage. Therefore, the husband's salary is considered to be the common property of the husband and wife. At the same time, the husband also owns an apartment, which is his separate property, and he rents it out for a monthly fee. Therefore, the monthly rent from the rent is considered to be the joint property of the husband and wife.
Prepared by: Mr. Seng Chhunly, Deputy Chairman of the Bar Association and Ms. Saing Sopheap, Member of the Working Group for the Preparation and Dissemination of Legal Articles of the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation
