Who is the newly appointed Commander-in-Chief of the Cambodian Armed Forces?
(Phnom Penh): On the afternoon of September 7, 2018, three senior officers of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces were promoted and officially announced for the new sixth mandate.
The three senior officers to be announced for the top positions in the Cambodian Armed Forces are: General Vong Pisen has been appointed Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, and General Ith Sarath has been appointed Deputy Commander-in-Chief. And Joint Chiefs of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Lieutenant General Hun Manet, Commander of the Army.
Of the three senior officers, one was the one who caused the most controversy and debate in the general public, especially in the army, the appointment of Lt. Gen. Vong Pisen as Commander-in-Chief. Of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
Prior to his role as Commander-in-Chief of the Nation, Lt. Gen. Vong Pisen has been the Deputy Commander and Chief of Staff of the National Gendarmerie since 2007.
Born in 1957 in Village 1, Kampong Leav Commune, Kampong Leav District, Prey Veng Province, Vong Pisen has a father named Mam Penh Vong as a teacher and a mother named Sek Yan as a housewife.
During the reign of former King Norodom Sihanouk and Lon Nol, Vong Pisen studied at Prey Veng High School. During the Pol Pot era, he was assigned to a mobile unit in Svay Daun Keo district, Pursat province. During the Cambodian state, he joined the army and is currently a senior officer of the National Police.
Coalition forces launch operation to crack down on illegal fishing in Koh Sotin district, confiscating and burning equipment
(Kampong Cham): Several joint police forces of Koh Sotin District Inspectorate, Lve Police Station, Special Intervention Force of Koh Sotin District Inspectorate and local authorities cooperated to crack down on fishing offenses at the former Lot 8 in Lve Srok Commune. Koh Sotin, Kampong Cham Province.
Major General Em Kosal, Provincial Police Commissioner of Kampong Cham Province, said that the operation took place on September 7, 2018, during which police confiscated nine nets, 880 meters long barriers, 210 bamboo poles and released them. 50 kg of mixed fish into the natural lake.
Major General Em Kosal emphasized that after receiving the information about the above illegal fishing offenses, he ordered the Koh Sotin District Police Inspector to send the relevant police forces to crack down on the crime immediately.
In fact, under the leadership of Brigadier General Lor Seang Huot, Deputy Inspector of the Koh Sotin District Police Inspectorate, and with the guidance of Mr. Phen Sophal, Governor of Koh Sotin District, the local joint police force also cooperated to operate the situation.
Commissioner Em Kosal said that after confiscating some exhibits that were illegal fishing gear, some exhibits with 250 meters of barricades and 210 bamboo poles were burned by the police on the spot.
Separately, 9 mosquito nets and 630 meters of barricades were temporarily stored at the Koh Sotin District Police Inspectorate to wait for further action.
