(China): Beijing has been collaborating with existing science areas in the city to help students set up their own businesses. Three entrepreneurial zones have been established over the past two years.
The area was established in collaboration with the Chongqing Chongqing Software Park by the Beijing Municipal Board of Education and other science parks. This area provides free business training services to current students and recent graduates up to 2 years.
A graduate of Tsinghua University, Yuan Jinlong was one of the first entrepreneurs to enter the software park area backed by Chongqing Chun Software Park. His team received assistance with project application, government funding, marketing and advertising, finance and other areas to start a business.
"The Entrepreneur Park area has a network with many enterprises that give us advice and even try out our products," said Yuan Jinlong, an entrepreneur. "This gives us a lot of good feedback and it helps us to develop our products well."
"We would like to invite all teachers or professionals to assist them in setting up a systematic training course, and we would like to invite all institutions to work together," said Lin Seangkui, manager of the Beijing Student Entrepreneur Park (Software Park). "Some high quality investments to invest in their projects as well."
With free assistance, Yuan's team has more than 20 institutions that apply their research results. His team has also won national high-tech enterprises and many other awards.
To date, these parks have served more than 2,000 students in 238 entrepreneurial groups. These areas also received 171 intellectual property patents. This is the emphasis of Mr. Lin Seang Kuy.
In particular, Mr. Xu Zhengwen, Deputy General Manager of Chongkunchun Software Park, said, "Currently, our main task is to invite third-party financial institutions to help fill the budget deficit so that these students can solve. Their new business plan.
"The next step is to raise funds for entrepreneurs and develop new technologies ourselves so that we can provide training and investment services to help these students "Grow their business bigger and stronger." (China's CCTV)
(China): A new technology that replaces the use of tap water when printing paper has been thoroughly evaluated by experts in southwest China's Yunnan Province recently. The technology was developed by an enterprise in Yunnan Province.
Its process not only contributes to the protection of the environment, but it is also a less expensive solution for the printing industry. China's newspaper and printing industry needs a lot of isopropyl alcohol to print. It releases a lot of waste water and gas.
It is estimated that the entire printing industry emits about 2.5 million tons of volatile organic compounds and 30,000 tons of waste, liquids and toxins annually.
Liu Hongpin, manager of the newspaper and printing department of Yunnan Newspaper Printing House, said that this technology is really a big change in the general paper printing technology. Compared to regular printing, this new technology is more quality. It improves many points, including spots on the surface of the paper and restores color quality.
