Secrets of ancient Angkor Wat construction and restoration by the Apsara Authority
(Siem Reap): After completing the renovation of the bank of Angkor Wat in the west corner of the north wing, which is 40 meters long at the end of 2017, was completed and in 2018, the experts of APSARA Authority are continuing to repair more. On the banks of Angkor Wat, the western corner of the same north wing, which is 20 meters long.
With this restoration work, Ms. Nong Boravy, Archaeologist of the Department of Preservation of Temples in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology of the APSARA Authority, who participated in the above process, also described some of the secrets of the construction of the banks of the Khmer ancestors of the Angkorian period and Describe the traditional and new techniques in repairing the banks of Angkor Wat.
Ms. Nong Boravy said that the 40-meter-long bank of Kasinthu, which was repaired, will take four years, starting from 2013-2017. Of these 40 meters, the first 20 meters were once repaired by the French in the 1960s, which can be seen through direct sight and clearer when the excavation pit was opened before repair. After the successful completion of the 40-meter project, the team also resumed the project to repair an additional 20 meters in 2018.
Ms. Boravy also stated that the main reasons why the structure of the Angkor Wat building on the west side of the north wing was severely damaged were due to the age of the construction, lack of regular maintenance, irregular water level fluctuations. Destroying the lower foundation, including the decay of the laterite layer below, large trees growing near the bank, interfering with the rock, causing the rock to move from its original position, leading to the collapse of the bank.
Currently, the APSARA Authority pays great attention to the preservation and restoration of both the temple and the shrine in order to preserve the ancient buildings, which are the symbols of the Khmer nation, to be strong, long-lasting and to enhance the beauty of the heritage buildings to be beautiful as a tourist attraction.
In addition, the training of human resources is also an important part that the APSARA Authority considers very much to increase its achievements without relying too much on international cooperation as before.
45 hectares of flooded forest land in Koh Kong province was confiscated by the perpetrators, authorities and environmental officials immediately cracked down
