(Phnom Penh): After waiting for more than a month after the election, the new National Assembly appeared on Wednesday, September 5, 2018, and is the day to see the presence of all 125 deputies. Photo from the Cambodian People's Party, which will serve the people as voters in the sixth mandate for 5 years.
Separately, for the next day, on September 6, the National Assembly will hold the election of the National Assembly locomotives, including: Chairman, Vice-President of the National Assembly, Chairman of the Vice-Chairman of the National Assembly Expert Committee, and also give confidence to the locomotives of the new government.
It should be noted that in the 6th National Assembly election, nearly 7 million people went to the polls, equivalent to more than 83% of the total population on the voter list, which has more than 8 million people. Among the people who went to the polls, the Cambodian People's Party (CPP) is the only political party that received the overwhelming majority of more than 4 million votes to control all 125 seats in the National Assembly.
A Brief History of the Cambodian Parliament:
The National Assembly of the Kingdom of Cambodia is the lower house of the 125-member House of Representatives, currently elected by proportional representation by universal suffrage. Each member of the National Assembly is elected for a five-year term, which is divided into representatives of the 25 provinces from 1 to 12 seats. A majority political party with at least 62 seats may have the right to form a government.
The election of members of the National Assembly, as well as the process of the National Assembly, also varies according to the regime in each period. According to the background of the National Assembly of Cambodia, which was recorded and published on the National Assembly website:
The People's Assembly of Democratic Kampuchea has 250 members from the same party. This parliament was elected on March 20, 1976.
The National Assembly of the People's Republic of Cambodia from 1981-1993 had 117 members from the same political party.
The 1993 Constituent Assembly has 120 members from multiple parties.
The 5th National Assembly from 2013 to the present has 123 members from multiple parties.
