The World Bank has decided to provide a $ 50 million loan to Cambodia, of which $ 20 million will be used to build a sewer system in Siem Reap.
On that occasion, Mr. Tea Seiha confirmed that in the past, there have been many study projects on sewer and wastewater in Siem Reap, and this meeting is also a capital for research on urban sewer system of the project. World Bank as well.
The Deputy Governor said that in the past, this historical cultural tourism city has always been flooded by rain, and later the APSARA Authority also built a flood protection dam in the upper part to avoid flooding in the city. However, when it rains, some areas in Siem Reap are still flooded due to lack of drainage system.
He said development partners for the sanitation sector include the Asian Development Bank, the Export-Import Bank and the French Development Agency. However, despite the support of development partners, Siem Reap still needs to build a sewerage and sub-sewerage network to connect people's homes to the existing sewerage network.
Mr. Ken Ratha said that in Siem Reap, according to the discharge of wastewater per day is about 25,000 cubic meters. Currently, this problem can be managed only 8,000 cubic meters per day, which is an issue that requires the support of development partners.
He added that the World Bank had recently decided to provide about $ 50 million in loans, including $ 20 million for water and sanitation projects to build sewers and sewers connected to homes. People go to the sewer network to collect wastewater from their homes and filter it at the wastewater treatment station before discharging it back to public water sources.
