According to the list of documents to be examined for 2018 by UNESCO, Cambodia has proposed to include the Wat Svay Andet Puppet Theater on the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity list, which Thailand urgently requires. Add Khon, a masked dance to the list of representatives
The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts of Cambodia announced that the document on the Puppet Theater was submitted to UNESCO on the morning of March 29, 2017 and received an official response from UNESCO later that afternoon.
Thai social media users seem to be very nationalistic and proud, claiming that the dance is exclusively owned and copyrighted by Thailand, not Cambodia. Angry Cambodians argue that the dance is part of Cambodian culture, while Thais claim it originated in their own country.
Cambodian Puppetry is a dance-drama based on the Reamker story, while Khmer poetry is based on the Ramayana in Sanskrit, and Thai Khon is believed to have originated from Khmer Puppetry. Khmer puppet shows were stolen during the Thai invasion and brought to Thailand to perform in the royal palace. The Thai form of Khon dance may have evolved from the stolen puppet theater.
Historically, Cambodia was a great empire when Thailand had not yet become a state, and the territory of Thailand today is part of the former Khmer Empire. Thailand seceded from Cambodia in the 13th century and has been a state ever since. Some Thai cultures and traditions originate in Cambodia.
Thai Khon dance is very similar to Khmer puppetry. Both dances are based on Indian poetry, the Ramayana, and feature the wearing of masks in performances with luxurious costumes, telling the story through dance, gestures and facial expressions. The two dances are similar but also remarkably different.
The people of both countries should understand that Cambodia and Thailand have a lot in common and should understand each other's culture, so that there will be no conflict over cultural issues. We can not prevent other countries from having a culture similar to ours, it is as if we can not prevent other people from wearing the same clothes as us.
Education about the true history and culture of peace is very important in the era of globalization. Educating the next generation to be racist and ultra-nationalist, as well as distort history, will create hostility and hatred among the people of neighboring countries and disparate communities.
For the Thai government and people, you should not distort the history of deceiving the next generation, you should acknowledge the truth and educate the people to respect the dignity and culture of the neighboring countries, as well as promote a culture of peace and a harmonious community.
It should be noted that according to UNESCO policies and procedures, each document will be reviewed and evaluated several times by an international panel of experts before being submitted for approval at a UNESCO Intergovernmental Committee meeting. Which is scheduled to take place at the end of 2018.
