(China): At least one Chinese garment maker is revising its overseas business plan to maintain market dominance while China and the United States are in a trade dispute.
"We have more than 1,000 employees in the assembly department," said Zhou Kang, head of overseas business management at Hart Group. "They produce about 2,000 sets of clothes a day, as much as possible."
With rising labor costs, Zhou says companies must be prepared for the worst.
"About 35 percent of our exports go to the United States," said Zhou Kang. We have already evaluated our marketing expansion plans and long-term relationships with our customers. "We will continue to follow our plan despite the trade dispute."
In order to make the company's products stand out from other products in the global market, the company is paying more attention to design.
"Good design enhances the added value of our products," says Hormin Huy, designer of Hart Group.
Ms. Ho admits that it takes a while for Chinese designers to catch up with the world's designers.
For now, the company is also capturing the potential of the domestic market, with people in China buying and using more products than ever before.
Today, Chinese consumers have more purchasing power because China has a larger middle class. And these people have become new target customers for companies that used to produce goods for export in the past. (China's CCTV) Chip Mong 271 Mega Mall creates huge job and business opportunities
