The first person to see the statue, Mr. Nget Oun, said that on Friday evening, he pioneered the forest behind the house to plant crops, suddenly found a stone statue buried in the ground, then he dug up and handed over Give Sambour Police Station to take care of.
Traing district governor Chhuon Phanny said that after the statue was kept at the Sambour police station, residents asked the authorities to take it to a religious festival to pray for peace. He added that on September 3, officials from the Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Takeo Province also accepted the statue to be kept at the Takeo Provincial Museum.
Meanwhile, Ms. Prum Sokuntheary, Director of the Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Takeo Province, stated that this statue is not a statue of Princess Liu Yi as the people say, but a "statue of Vishnu", she claimed that the statue above looks Like a large statue of Vishnu taken from Phnom Da to be kept at the National Museum in Phnom Penh.
This is a mistake of the director of the Takeo Provincial Department of Culture, as she did not see the statue in person, only through the communication network that the deputy director of the provincial department sent to her. The director of the department seems to be indifferent and does not take this information seriously to report to the management as she is the director of the national cultural department of the whole province.
Of course, this deity statue is purely feminine, not Vishnu, the male deity, only having four arms like Vishnu and a tubular crown resembling the Vishnu crown of the same period. The statue has large breasts and hips that look like a virgin and is adorned with long skirts.
This misunderstanding and question was clarified by Professor Chen Chanratana, President of the Khmer Heritage Foundation and an expert on archeology and art history. »A female deity with great power in Brahmanism.
According to many years of observation and research, he confirmed that according to the decorative design of the Nokor Phnom period statue, which has its capital in Angkor Borei (now in Takeo province), it was built around 6th or 7th century.
