Floods and landslides caused by heavy rains in central and northern Vietnam in recent weeks have killed a total of 13 people and left three missing. . This was confirmed by the Vietnam Disaster Prevention and Management Authority, quoted by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Monday afternoon, September 3, 2018.
In addition, floods and landslides also destroyed 364 homes, damaged more than 6,523 hectares of farmland, killed 512 cattle and 56,367 poultry, and affected 963 aquaculture lakes. Hectares and dams 620 meters long with a canal of about 6,174 meters.
From January to August 2018, natural disasters, mostly in the form of typhoons, floods and landslides, killed or left 153 people injured, 119 injured and damaged. Public property is worth more than $ 304 million. Mr. Ith Sam Heng: Discussion on raising the minimum wage for workers in 2019 will be completed before Pchum Ben
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training and Chairman of the Labor Advisory Committee, confirmed that the discussion on raising the minimum wage in 2019 for workers in the textile, garment and footwear sector will be completed before the festival. Pchum Ben is coming, as each party has expressed hope that the talks will run smoothly.
This confirmation was made after a preliminary meeting of the 18th 8th Mandate Labor Advisory Committee at the Ministry of Labor on the morning of September 3, 2018 to determine the tripartite composition and the date of the wage discussion. Coming soon.
Minister Ith Sam Heng said that this tripartite mechanism, like the previous one, was limited to 16 parties, meaning 16 representatives of the Royal Government, 16 representatives of employers and six representatives of unions. He said that the meeting to discuss the minimum wage will start from September 10, 2018 onwards.
He said the meeting also highlighted the socio-economic situation as well as the seven criteria related to the textile, garment and footwear sectors, as well as the minimum wage situation in the country and its competitors, which are all inputs for members. The Labor Advisory Committee also considers.
He added that for 2018, the debate will run smoothly, in a spirit of mutual understanding and adhere to the discussion strategy, and continue to discuss until a figure can be found that is close to each other and there is a minimum gap for final approval.
According to Nang Sothy, Deputy Chairman of the Labor Advisory Committee (Employers' Representative), the employers expect that the Ministry of Labor, which represents the Royal Government, will set a reasonable price and accept both employers and unions to keep their jobs. The better. Employers are also pleased with the unrest in the workplace, which is less common than before.
Mr. Nang Sothy continued that while the Royal Government, the Employers' Association (GMAC) is trying to train professional technical skills for workers, see that productivity in the textile, garment and footwear sector has improved, and buyers also have Switch from buying low-priced items to buying high-priced items.
At this point, Mr. Nang Sothy seems to want to emphasize that the increase in the minimum wage for workers in 2019 is not a problem, although the payment of workers 'compensation and workers' insurance is a 100 percent burden on employers and factory owners. It is also important that the Royal Government ensure security in the workplace.
Please be informed that the minimum wage of workers in the textile, garment and footwear sector in Cambodia is gradually increasing every year, and in 2018, the minimum wage of workers in this sector increased to 170 US dollars per month. . Chinese president: China's financial aid is not a debt trap
