(Prey Veng): Mr. Sar Sokha, Member of Parliament for Prey Veng Constituency and Vice President of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia, presided over the opening ceremony of the scholarship exam to study in China on the morning of September 1, 2018 at Preah Ang Duong High School in Prey Veng Province.
Mr. Aing Serey Piseth, Chairman of the Scholarship Management Team to Study in China, said that this morning, September 1, Prey Veng Province opened the second exam session, which was organized by the province with 20 students. 10 civil engineering engineers and 10 international business students to study at Hefei University in Anhui Province, People's Republic of China.
"There are four subjects in this exam: Mathematics, Physics, English and General Knowledge," he said. "This year is tighter than last year because of the quality of these two exam subjects."
"Under the leadership and development of human resources, as well as the contribution to the implementation of the Rectangular Strategy, the third phase of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the sixth legislature of the National Assembly aims to improve the knowledge and skills of young people to have sufficient capacity to improve their livelihood to participate. "Restore the society to keep up with global trade, both regionally and globally."
In fact, this scholarship has been established since the 5th mandate, in 2013, and the National Scholarship students of Prey Veng, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey have chosen the following skills: English, Accounting, Marketing Management, Information Technology, Banking, Economy, Tourism, International Relations. And engineers and so on.
The team coordinated and sent students to a total of 14 schools, universities and educational institutions in 2017 and plans to send students to more than 18 partner universities, institutes and higher education institutions in the provinces and Phnom Penh in the future. A total of 1165 faces.
Mr. Ang Serey Piseth said that under the leadership of Mr. Sar Sokha, scholarships were given to 53 students from Prey Veng, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey to study higher education for 4 years at Hefei University in Anhui Province, People's Republic of China. 8 females.
Immigration police continue to detain 17 Vietnamese nationals who smuggled across the border to stay and work illegally in Sen Sok district
(Phnom Penh): The Joint Operations Team of the Investigation and Enforcement Department of the General Department of Immigration this morning, September 1, 2018, continued to inspect the target of foreign workers' dormitories on Street 2002, Sangkat Teuk Thla, Khan Sen Sok, Phnom Penh. Accommodation of foreigners.
The director of the Investigation and Enforcement Department, Lt. Gen. Ouk Haysila, said that this morning's operation was in cooperation with the Phnom Penh Municipal Police under the coordination of Mr. Seang Sok, Deputy Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court.
According to the director general of the Department of Special Forces, 17 foreigners, including six Vietnamese women, were found to be doing business and staying without documents, smuggling into Cambodia illegally.
After the arrest, the operation team brought the 17 Vietnamese nationals to the General Department of Immigration for questioning and further proceedings.
Aung San Suu Kyi was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1991 for her efforts against the former Myanmar military regime for peace, non-violence and democracy, and she has become a symbol of democracy both internationally and in Myanmar.
But after the violence in Rakhine State erupted in August 2017, leaving thousands of Rohingya dead and more than 700,000 fleeing to Bangladesh, Suu Kyi's popularity plummeted. Some even asked the Nobel Committee to revoke the Nobel Peace Prize.
In addition, a UN report released on Monday condemned her for failing to act as a state adviser in resolving the Rohingya crisis, but instead supporting the Myanmar military's military operation in Rakhine state. Back.
However, the Norwegian Nobel Committee has said that the UN report on the Rohingya genocide in Myanmar's Rakhine state does not qualify for the revocation of the Nobel Peace Prize by Aung San Suu Kyi. .
"It is important to remember that the Nobel Peace Prize, whether in physics, literature or peace, is given only for past efforts and achievements," the committee said. "The revocation of the Nobel Prize is not under the rule of the Nobel Committee."
