Thai Norak Satya, spokesman for the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, told Fresh News on the afternoon of August 31, 2018 that the ministry had asked UNESCO to include the "Puppet Theater" as an intangible world heritage site for more than a year. As usual, UNESCO may announce whether or not to add it to the World Heritage List later this year.
Mr. Thai Noraksatya said that Cambodia has high hopes of inserting the "Puppet Theater" on the World Heritage List because Cambodia has submitted a large number of documents, both open and secret, in order to comply with UNESCO statutes.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts confirmed: "Actually, we have been hoping since the first day, if not hope, we will not ask. "We have done our best in our past experience. The Minister has put a lot of effort into submitting documents as required by UNESCO."
Mr. Thai Noraksatya also explained that UNESCO rejects any request unless the submitting party fails to complete the documents, documents and documents in accordance with UNESCO law or any country protests. As for Cambodia's "Puppet Theater" proposal, no country has come forward to protest since the day it was launched.
The subject of the inclusion of the "Puppet Theater" on the Intangible World Heritage List is sparking a flurry of reactions on Facebook and on social media between Thais and Thai people claiming ownership of the property. This one culture.
It is remembered that in 2003, because of the anger that came from the Thai actor called "Morning Star" talking about the reputation of the temple of Angkor, the Khmer people erupted in anger to burn the Thai embassy and beat Destroy the assets of Thai companies in Cambodia as well. Finally, the losers of Cambodia have to pay the damages of the Thai Embassy.
Concerned about the ownership of the "Puppet Theater" between Cambodia and Thailand as violent as in 2003, Thai Noraksatya called on Cambodians to remain calm and not try to provoke any anger. That can lead to violence and resentment.
Of the 22 cities in China, Beijing has won the overall business environment assessment. This is according to a joint report released by the National Committee for Development and Reform of China and Beijing City Hall on Monday.
According to the report, Beijing has put in place a number of policies and measures to support reforms, such as opening up businesses and issuing construction permits, which have greatly improved the city's business environment.
In addition, Beijing has included 26 reforms and 136 policy lists to improve the business environment in the city, of which 88 policies have been implemented.
The Comprehensive Administrative Service Center in Beijing's Haitian District provides one-stop-shop services with on-site staff at the tax department, public security department and other industrial and commercial buildings to facilitate access to services.
Zhaoyang County is the first district in Beijing to offer part-time and extended overtime services for high-demand services, with two working hours in addition to normal working hours to meet the needs of those in need. The better. Locals can also apply online to book service hours every Saturday.
Chinese lawmakers on Monday began reviewing a draft law on land pollution prevention and control.
The draft law was submitted to the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress for final discussion and vote on the draft law at a meeting from Monday to Friday.
The draft law on pollution prevention and control was submitted to the Chinese National Assembly for its first discussion in June last year.
After two deliberations, the draft law also emphasizes the prevention of land pollution and land protection.
"The draft law emphasizes prevention at the source," said Hu Kharming, vice chairman of the National People's Congress (NPC) Constitution and Law Committee. "Companies and individuals that produce, use, store, transport, retrieve and dispose of toxic substances should take effective measures to prevent them from leaking or spreading to avoid soil pollution."
The draft law also legally defines the responsibilities of polluters, stating that polluters have an obligation to manage pollution risks as well as rehabilitate soil fertility.
The draft law also provides for stricter penalties for offenders by increasing fines for violating the law.
"The draft law stipulates that companies participating in land situation statistics, risk assessments and outcome assessments will be banned from conducting business if found "That those companies make fake reports."
The draft law stipulates that there will be various national standards for managing the risk of soil pollution. And a national land condition statistic should be done at least once every 10 years.
A network of monitoring stations will be set up across the country, and the data and information obtained will be shared with authorities working on the environment, agriculture, natural resources, housing, water resources, health, forestry and wilderness.
The environmental and health authorities of China's central government are required to inspect and evaluate toxic and hazardous substances in the soil and make a list of those substances for public release.
According to the draft law, the central and provincial governments should budget for the prevention and control of land pollution.
