(China): China is integrating more and more smart technologies to increase the efficiency of home care services for the elderly.
With China's health sector undergoing a technological revolution in recent years, China has now begun to develop robots for the elderly. Some of the latest series of robots were in the spotlight at last week's three-day Guangzhou Elderly Health Expo.
Equipped with the latest advanced face recognition system, a robot is capable of examining the human body and prescribing according to the needs of the user.
"It is a comprehensive health management system with data transmission for both patients and doctors," said Zhang Chhun, general manager of Thonglor Guangzhou Information Technology Company. "If the health condition of the elderly is abnormal, the device will immediately send the data to our health center to send help and rescue them."
In Shanghai, local governments are also using a home water monitoring system to record the amount of water that is immediately used in each apartment.
The system's creators say that by checking the water data sent to their online management system, it is possible to know if the elderly person is safe and living normally while he or she is at home.
Volunteers in the community will be sent to check if the water use of the elderly has changed.
Ruling party spokesman: Dogs only bark, people only land?
(Phnom Penh): The spokesman of the ruling party, Mr. Sok Isan, on August 30, 2018, considered the propaganda of the opposition to prevent activities in Cambodia to the Khmer proverb that "dogs always bark at people "Just land."
The ruling party's spokesman made the remarks after fleeing opposition leader Sam Rainsy and some opposition groups propagandized that Kem Sokha would be released today. Or propaganda that there will be political negotiations in the future, including the expectation of a re-election.
Mr. Sok Isan explained: "A few days ago, some people said from word of mouth that when the court opens the case of Kem Sokha on 30 August 18, Kem Sokha will be released. They went to wait to welcome Kem Sokha. "But in reality, there is no release like the rumors and analysis of the tendency to want to be released. Kem Sokha's case is still in the court process."
"Some opposition groups say there will be political talks in the near future to revive the former opposition party," he said. "But in reality, things are not as it should be, because the former opposition party was dissolved by the Supreme Court and the final verdict was closed on November 16, 2017."
A CPP spokesman added: "The Senate election and the 6th legislative election have been successfully held and there is no going back. "So the tendency to want both elections to go back is no way."
Please note that after winning the 6th National Assembly election, the Cambodian People's Party, which won all 125 seats in the National Assembly, will hold its first National Assembly meeting on September 5, 2018. In the near future, the Royal Government of the 6th mandate, which continues to be led by the Cambodian People's Party, will also be formed on September 6, 2018.
Mr. Sok Isan added that "the first meeting of the National Assembly to prepare the engine of the National Assembly and the trust of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the sixth legislature will continue as planned without any changes."
