(China): The Chinese government will look into some issues related to public school institutions across China by the end of this year to reduce the huge pressure that those institutions place on students. This is according to the Chinese Ministry of Education on Thursday last week.
As of Aug. 20, 382,000 non-public educational institutions across China were inspected. Of those, 45,000 schools were found to have rectified student care issues. This is according to Lu Yukang, director of the Department of Primary Education at the Chinese Ministry of Education.
Luo said the Chinese Ministry of Education will address any issues that put additional pressure on many students, including teaching lessons that are beyond the normal level of student age, as well as over-concern with teaching. Just to pass the exam.
The move comes after China's central government administration issued guidelines last Wednesday aimed at tightening controls on institutions that provide part-time or off-campus education services.
Chinese education officials have stressed that local education authorities must adhere to the guidelines and raise the standard of setting up part-time public schools in their areas as soon as possible and plan. And schedule as precisely as necessary to address past issues.
Lu Yukang, director of the Department of Elementary Education at the Chinese Ministry of Education, said: "As for the criteria for determining which part-time teaching of public schools is out of the curriculum, we will gather relevant experts to monitor and analyze. On the teaching schedule as well as the content of those courses. "Therefore, we must take effective measures to point the way to these educational institutions."
The Chinese Ministry of Education has also said it will advise local authorities on the provision of part-time services instead of overtime or appropriate classes for elementary and middle school students. The ministry will also deepen teaching reform to improve classroom efficiency and quality. (China's CCTV)
