India tests first biofuel aircraft
NEW DELHI: India's first biofuel-powered aircraft officially flew a test flight on Monday. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Monday afternoon, August 27, 2018.
The biofuel for the Q400 test flight was developed by the Dehradun-based State Institute of Fuel and Agricultural Waste. In addition, if the test is successful, India will join a group of nations, including the United States and Australia, as aviation biofuels.
Minister of Agriculture holds an emergency meeting to resolve issues for farmers affected by natural disasters in a timely manner
(Phnom Penh): Received an order from Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister, on August 23, this morning, August 27, 2018, Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, chaired an internal meeting of the Ministry of Emergency. One to solve problems for farmers in time at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
The above meeting of the Ministry of Agriculture is organized for four main purposes, including:
First, summarize the impact on mixed crops, especially agro-industrial crops, especially rice and livestock, which have been damaged by natural disasters (floods and droughts) in the affected provinces.
Second, plan the immediate intervention as necessary to repair the damage.
Third, study the needs of farmers and the availability of the Ministry's supply, such as rice, seeds, all kinds of crops and pesticides.
Fourth, the introduction of a number of measures to be taken to minimize the amount of smoke sitting.
At the same time, the Ministry also summarized the report and submitted it to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, for review and requested measures to solve the problem for the farmers in a timely and efficient manner.
(Pakistan): Chinese and Pakistani agricultural researchers have achieved great success in breeding high-quality wheat varieties through the use of Chinese double-row wheat varieties in Pakistan since last year. 2016 is coming.
In November 2016, in order for Pakistan to meet all the huge needs of wheat as the country's most important crop, these scientists began experimenting with growing Chinese hybrid wheat and discovered the true nature of nature. Of wheat grains in a laboratory at the University of Peshawar.
"This is my farm where I grow hybrid wheat from the Beijing Engineering Research Center in China," said Mohammad Arif, a pilot farm farmer at Pesawa University. We tested this hybrid wheat on this farm. Behind me is a field where we grow rice in rows. "Most of these crops are very productive."
Despite rapid success, these researchers are still trying to find new ways to make these wheat better adapted to the environment here, as well as to protect against pests and diseases.
"So hybrid wheat has the ability to withstand all of these problems and can more than double the yield of wheat and ensure food security," said Iqbal Munor, director of the Institute of Biotechnology and Genetic Engineering at Pesawa University. "For our country and for the whole world."
Pakistan has seen an increase in wheat production and agricultural technology after the introduction of several Chinese hybrid wheat varieties. These hybrid wheat varieties have also developed better in terms of their immunity and superiority compared to other wheat varieties.
