(Phnom Penh): Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Institution has already received a permit from the National Bank of Cambodia for the issuance of corporate bonds. This is according to the posting on the official website of Hattha Kaksekar Microfinance Institution today.
Mr. Oknha Huot Ieng Tong stated, “I am very proud that the National Bank of Cambodia has issued a letter of objection to the issuance of HKL Corporate Bonds at this time. We will be the first company in the history of Cambodia to issue bonds. ”
Hattha Kaksekar stated that the first corporate bond in Cambodia No. 1 will be issued by HKL and will be offered for sale with a total principal of 80 billion Riels with the possibility of selling up to 40 billion Riels in case of excess demand. From target investors. Corporate bonds have a term of three years and the annual coupon rate of the bonds is from 7.50% to 8.50%.
What is a bond?
One of the main differences between stocks and bonds is that bonds represent ownership and equity represents the ownership of a company. Stock prices can rise rapidly if the company grows and becomes more profitable. Bonds, on the other hand, pay only the pre-determined interest rates and their prices fluctuate within the limits set by the interest rate, not the profitability of the company.
Currently, only one type of corporate bond is allowed to be listed on the Cambodia Securities Exchange due to the lack of a state bond. Corporate bonds are a type of bond issued by a limited liability public company or a legal entity.
According to the Prakas on the Issuance of Public Bonds issued by the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia, corporate bonds can be divided into:
