In 2017, out of 5.6 million international tourists to Cambodia, more than 1.2 million were Chinese tourists, an increase of 46%, ranking first. In the first six months of 2018, Cambodia received more than 930,000 Chinese tourists, an increase of 76% and a market share of about 31% of Cambodia's international tourists, and currently Cambodia has 1,046 Chinese-speaking tour guides in the province. Siem Reap has 851 people.
Philippine President Duterte says he will not buy F-16 fighter jets after US insults
MANILA: Philippine President Rodrigo Duterte has blamed the United States for trying to block military modernization and for criticizing his country's drug crackdown, and has now vowed not to buy F-16 fighter jets. Not from America. This is according to a quote from the Russian newspaper Sputnik on Friday afternoon, August 24, 2018.
Duterte called the defense chief's and top US officials' request for him to buy F-16 fighter jets "useless" because his country needed lighter aircraft to fight the rebels. The Philippine president's remarks refer to letters he received from US Secretary of Defense James Mattis, US Secretary of State Maipopo, and US Secretary of Commerce Veal Buros.
Washington has so far refused to hand over some 23,000 firearms to Philippine police after some US officials expressed concern over Manila's anti-drug campaign. Washington has also warned the Manila government not to buy sophisticated military equipment from Russia.
Responding to the request of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Microfinance Association of Cambodia told the members to examine the possibility of actual repayment and provide a flexible mechanism for flood victims
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Microfinance Association (CFA) issued a statement on August 24, 2018, confirming the outcome of an emergency meeting of the association regarding the various mechanisms of lending of microfinance institutions. Members to flood victims.
This emergency meeting was held after August 23, 2018, Samdech Techo Hun Sen told the National Bank of Cambodia and the Ministry of Economy and Finance to work with banking and microfinance institutions to find a way to Reduce repayment periods or reduce interest rates for people who have borrowed money from those institutions and suffered floods.
The statement of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia stated that "On August 23, 2018, the Board of Directors and the management of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia held an emergency meeting to discuss the development and impact of natural disasters, especially Concerned about floods in the provinces along the Mekong River and rising water levels in some other provinces that could affect the lives and livelihoods of Cambodians.
However, the Cambodian Microfinance Association will continue to monitor the situation and the potential impact, as well as be prepared to participate in appropriate mechanisms to alleviate the burden of the people who receive. Seriously directly affected by natural disasters to their livelihoods and livelihoods.
In addition, the Cambodian Microfinance Association would like to call on and encourage all microfinance institutions and member institutions to perform the following additional tasks:
1: Continue to provide credit carefully, examining the possibility of actual repayment and provide a soft settlement mechanism for customers who are severely affected by floods.
2. Continue to monitor the situation and impact of natural disasters on MFI clients by providing the necessary support in accordance with the actual circumstances and reporting to the Cambodian Microfinance Association in a timely manner.
The Cambodian Microfinance Association hopes and believes that all microfinance institutions and related partners will cooperate to provide up-to-date information and take appropriate measures to support the people in this difficult situation. DaraPay Launches "Electronic Wallet" to Modernize Cash Payment Services in Cambodia!
