(China): 95% of Chinese students who study abroad are looking for job opportunities in China. This is according to a job report on overseas students released in Beijing on Sunday.
The report shows that most Chinese students who study abroad find a job half a year after they return to China, and they understand that the country's labor market is very promising. Better results.
Students born after the 1990s make up 55% of the group of students who return home after graduating abroad.
According to the report, 76 percent of Chinese students study abroad because they want to experience different cultures and expand their horizons, rather than travel abroad to live.
"It is very difficult for us to stay in the UK because of the country's secession from the European Union," said Zhang Pengli, a graduate student at the University of Cambridge in the United Kingdom. And it is not easy to get a job or a promotion even if we are there. "So I decided to come back because there are definitely a lot of opportunities in China for my career."
According to the report, 72 percent of Chinese returning students receive job opportunities. Another 16 percent are looking for work and 5 percent are starting their own business. Most of them will get a job within six months.
