The rapid growth of e-commerce has had a negative impact on the supermarket sector, which has led to a sharp decline in profits due to more mobile shopping combined with a wider range of internet coverage. Although many national and international supermarket companies in China have plummeted during this period, Parkinson is still growing.
In April 2016, Parkson opened Line Shopping Mall in the eastern Chinese city of Qingdao, the first self-built shopping mall in China.
For most retailers, it is impossible to ignore a market of 1.3 billion people, said Son Chung Sui-hyung, CEO and CEO of Parkinson Retail Group. China has grown rapidly since China opened its markets in the early 1990s. The purchasing power and income levels of local consumers are recovering rapidly. "That's why we have so much confidence in this market."
By adjusting its product mix, sales at Parkinson's in China rebounded 1.3 percent in 2017 after declining 6.7 percent in 2016.
"We focus on cosmetics, youth clothing and sportswear because we understand that these points can attract young customers to our market," said Son Chung Sui Hyung.
The company said that as China gradually reduces import tariffs, the selling price of goods will fall, thus boosting demand. Parkinson is also launching its online marketing campaign to attract customers to its mall.
