"Since ASEAN Plus Three, import tariffs have been reduced or reduced to zero, so many Malaysian products have entered China and have the advantage," said Levi Keung, Managing Director of Regal Plus. Awesome. Later, Malaysian products were also in high demand after the free trade agreement. "So our products like coffee and biscuits are selling like hot cakes since then."
From coffee to ice cream, sales of durian products and durian products make the company half of its total revenue.
Despite this, competition in the low-cost food sector remains difficult, both for well-known international companies and for local companies that tend to cut costs.
The biggest challenge, he says, is the rapidly changing consumer attitudes and marketing methods.
"For young customers, they like to shop on the phone," he said. We must have our own software for customers to make purchases over the phone. So we have no choice but to join the WeChat store. Of course, we spend a lot of money to advertise our shopping program. "Even though the market is very good, we have to spend a lot of money to keep up with the market."
Regal still has high confidence in China, which still considers China a very potential market.
"Third and fourth tier cities are where purchasing power is developing today," said Levy Keung, managing director of Regal Plus. In the past, they were able to buy only cheap local products. But now they want products imported from abroad. If we talk about food safety and its taste. "On the other hand, because China has a one- or two-child policy, Chinese people always want to provide the best food for their children."
Many Malaysian fruits, such as durian or durian-based products, are eaten by millions of Chinese.
Despite growing trade tensions and rising wages in China, Lu plans to open a factory in China to deliver the company's goods to the market sooner. (China's CCTV)
