25 Cambodian and International Youth Volunteers Completed Internships Related to World Heritage
(Siem Reap): 25 trainees, including 10 from APSARA Authority, Preah Vihear National Authority, Sabo Prei Kuk National Authority, Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia and 15 others from 9 countries around the world on August 20, 2018. Received a certificate of completion after successfully completing the training course on "World Heritage Volunteer Youth Education".
At the closing ceremony, Mr. Hang Pov thanked the relevant institutions that organized this program, as well as all the participants who took the time to study during the past 10 days. He hopes that through this training, participants will learn more about Angkor culture, civilization and community conservation and the Angkor Code of Conduct.
He said that the 10 days of the participants could not be enough to study and understand the whole Angkor resort, apart from the temples, there is an intangible heritage of the locals, but the participants really know and know some of the celebrations. Residents during the study day, the Angkor area has preserved the cultural landscape very well.
Ms. Sin Boram, Director of the Korean Cultural Heritage Institute and organizer of the event, said that this is the second time that a training course has been held in the Angkor area in collaboration with UNESCO, the Apsara Authority, the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia and the Korea-Angkor Institute for Heritage. About cultural heritage.
She hopes that this training course will be available in the coming years for young Cambodians and young people around the world to better understand the conservation and development of Angkor. She stated that in the future, this training course will not only be held in the Angkor area, but also in Preah Vihear and Sambo Prey Kuk provinces, and we would like to ask all young people to participate as much as possible.
