(Phnom Penh): Police raided an illegal drug distribution site called "Raksmey Sour" by confiscating 500 cases of drugs, requesting more than 1.3 million pills in Trong Moan village, Sangkat Obek Kam, Khan Sen Sok, Phnom Penh. This is according to the press release of the Committee to Combat Counterfeit Products that pose a high risk to health and social safety, which was received by Fresh News on the afternoon of August 18, 2018.
According to the press release, the crackdown was carried out on August 16, 2018 by the Special Task Force of the Anti-Counterfeit Products Committee, led by Major General Touch Yuthea, Deputy Secretary of the Secretariat, in cooperation with the specialized forces and local forces. Under the coordination of Deputy Prosecutor Ngin Pich.
The Anti-Counterfeit Products Committee confirmed that the location of the crackdown was "Raksmey Sour" Pharmaceutical Import and Distribution Company, located at 217, Street 1019, Trong Moan Village, Sangkat O Bek Kam, Khan Sen Sok, with a company representative named Hor Chhun. At house No. 350, Street 173, Sangkat Olympic, Khan Chamkarmon, who is stealing and distributing non-legal medicinal products.
With the coordination of the representative of the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, the inspection force found: 23 types of drugs, a total of 500 cases equal to 1,329,620 tablets and 69,105 packages, and a total of 12 types of expired drugs. 20 cases.
After reviewing, the joint committee concluded that the offenses such as: suspected of being the location of the packaging of counterfeit drugs and validating drugs, storing and distributing counterfeit and expired drug products, without a permit to distribute drugs and operating companies do not comply with the law. News Update: Indian Army is burning hands and feet to evacuate 10,000 people to safety
NEW DELHI: The Indian military is stepping up rescue operations for more than 10,000 people stranded by floods in the southern Indian state of Kerala as the death toll has risen to 324. More than 200,000 people are homeless. This is according to the release by the BBC on Saturday, August 18, 2018.
The Indian government has decided to send hundreds of troops to join the rescue operation, backed by dozens of boats and helicopters that have been evacuating people stranded by the floods. Get safe. But at the same time, the Indian Meteorological Authority also forecast that strong winds and rain are likely to continue on Saturday and Sunday.
