Immediately after the news that the lira had depreciated, photos were posted showing crowds lined up at luxury stores in Turkey. The depreciation of the Turkish lira has made luxury goods here one of the cheapest in the world, as some brands have not adjusted their prices in time. Luxury prices here are estimated to be about 25 percent cheaper after the lira depreciated.
"Some of our customers and tour guides in Istanbul want to buy luxury goods, but they have to wait in line for a long time," said Han Li, Ctrip's Chief Business Officer for the Middle East and Africa. "We will reschedule our tour to Turkey to add more time to the mall if most customers need it."
In response to this growing demand, some luxury stores have adjusted their prices, while others are restricting one visitor to one. Tour companies have also shut down the lira payment service to prevent the purchase of lira for sale when it rises again in the future.
So far, there has been no significant change in booking trips to Turkey. But overall, the number of Chinese tourists visiting here is now the largest compared to three years ago, as Turkey has been heavily promoting its tourism industry.
