Samdech Techo Hun Sen vows to serve the people not to be disappointed and continue to develop Cambodia to be more prosperous after being appointed by the King (video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia on the morning of August 18 This year 2018 is committed to serve the people not to be disappointed and continue to strive to develop Cambodia in this new mandate to grow even more after receiving the order from the King.
In a message on Facebook, Samdech Techo Hun Sen affirmed: "I am determined to serve the people not to be disappointed and will continue to strive to build the development of our country to grow. You must remember that the CPP belongs to all of us and the victory of the CPP is the common success of Cambodia as a whole. I wish you all a happy family reunion on the weekend. ”
Please be informed that on August 17, 2018, His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of the Kingdom of Cambodia, issued a Royal Decree appointing Samdech Techo Hun Sen as the Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia for a new term. After the Cambodian People's Party won the election by a landslide. Samdech Techo Hun Sen is the ruling party's only prime ministerial candidate.
Article 2 of the Royal Decree directs Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia, to organize the composition of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
However, the new National Assembly will convene for the first time on September 5, 2018, and the new government will be formed on September 6, 2018.
Residents of Roka Kong 2 commune, Kandal province, insist on the authorities to help open a dirt road into the village because one family stole the land and blocked the road
(Kandal): Many families in the back village in Prek Chrov village, Roka Kong 2 commune, Muk Kampoul district, Kandal province, insisted that the authorities open a dirt road to their village so that they can travel and transport agricultural products. . The reason for this insistence is because a family has stolen a house on the road and abused it as their own land.
Bou Kim, 77, insisted that the authorities help clear the road, but she did not want to hear anything.
A woman named Sam Chantha also stated that the reason why the couple dared to move the house to encroach on the road by the landlord was based on a land title deed that completely violated the road on August 23, 2011. It is bordered on the east by Bukim and Phal Thol. The woman added that the land, named Bou Kim and Phal Thol, was actually east of the trail, and that Bou Kim and Phal Thol confirmed that they did not have their thumbprints on the documents.
Regarding this request, Mr. Poch Ly Srun, Roka Kong 2 commune chief, told by phone on the afternoon of August 15, 2018 that this was beyond his ability because both parties had already filed a lawsuit. The landlord is negotiating to buy land named Bou Kim and Phal Thol half a meter to widen the road to end the story.
The commune chief confirmed that this road has existed since the old society, and in 2013, Mr. Mao Phirun, the governor of Kandal province, donated many stones to build and repair this road.
Over the years, we have come a long way in improving and developing Sino-Japanese relations. Therefore, the long-term adjustment of Sino-Japanese relations has led to new developments, especially as the two countries have received new opportunities to boost economic and trade ties.
The Sino-Japanese relationship over the past 40 years has emerged with the greatest feature being that the economic and trade ties between the two countries are inseparable. Since 2007, China has been Japan's largest trading partner for 11 consecutive years. It can be said that Chinese goods such as food, clothing, sports goods and children's items have penetrated into the daily life of the Japanese people.
In the case of Japanese supermarkets and food enterprises, Chinese production means high quality and low cost, which can help manage capital. In the first half of this year, more than 4 million Chinese tourists visited Japan, ranking first among Japanese foreign tourists. Experts say Japan is starting to rely on the Chinese economy.
