About 300 people from Kandeang district, Pursat province visit the Khmer Rouge tribunal to learn about the trial process
(Phnom Penh): About 300 people from Kandeang district, Pursat province, visited the Khmer Rouge tribunal on August 16, 2018 to learn about the trial of the former Khmer Rouge leader. Killed at least 1.7 million Cambodians through torture, starvation, drug abuse, internal purges, torture, murder and other inhumane acts.
Neth Pheaktra, head of public affairs and spokesman for the Khmer Rouge tribunal, said that most of the people who visited the study were victims of the Khmer Rouge regime and were very interested in the trial process.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra explained to the people the importance of establishing the Khmer Rouge tribunal as a result of the cooperation between the Royal Government of Cambodia and the United Nations. The UN-Cambodia Joint Tribunal was set up to hear two groups of people: former senior leaders and those responsible for serious crimes.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra pointed out that from 2009 to August 2018, there were more than 600,000 Cambodians, half of whom were victims of the Khmer Rouge regime and half of whom were the younger generation. He stated that this was a great success for the Khmer Rouge tribunal, which received overwhelming support from the public compared to other international courts.
This promotion was mentioned by Mr. Neth Pheaktra that it is very important to strengthen the understanding of the people, especially the younger generation to understand the history of the Khmer Rouge regime. After completing many tasks in the old mandate, Minister Ith Sam Heng urges subordinates to work hard for the new mandate.
