Ministry of Rural Development organizes training of trainers on "Road Safety" to raise awareness, educate, disseminate and enforce the law
The training was conducted under the chairmanship of Mr. Song Sophal, Project Manager, Rural Road Improvement Phase 2, and Dr. Masato Koto, Chairperson of the Road Safety Technical Advisory Group, with 48 participants from the Ministry and the Department of Rural Development. .
This training course is designed to raise the awareness of traffic safety to the officers who carry out education, dissemination and promotion of respect and implementation of the law on road safety.
The Road Safety Trainer Training Manual for this course is specially designed for community education and will focus on speeding, driving under the influence of alcohol, wearing a helmet, pedestrian safety, cycling safety, school safety, rescue. Primary and training facilitation methods.
Speaking at the training, Mr. Song Sophal said that of course, if there is a good road, the transportation and travel of people will be easier and faster, but at the same time the risk of accidents Traffic is also rising.
Therefore, he said, educating, disseminating and improving traffic safety to the people on the project road is a necessary task to remind all citizens to be careful, respect and implement the traffic law consistently, and continue to disseminate to family members and children. Together with the villagers and the students in the village, be very careful to protect yourself from traffic accidents.
Dr. Masato Koto also said that if there are no specific measures to prevent and reduce traffic accidents, the number of road deaths in Cambodia will increase to 3,200 by 2020.
Dr. asserted that the Community Road Safety Program of the second phase of the Rural Road Improvement Project is a major contribution and in line with the National Road Safety Plan 2011-2020 of the Royal Government of Cambodia, committed to reducing the number of fatalities. 50% by 2020.
According to the Road Traffic Accident and Victim Information System, nearly 2,000 people are killed and nearly 5,000 seriously injured in road accidents each year.
According to the Provisional Report of the Road Traffic Accident and Victim Information System 2017, compared to 100,000 people in Cambodia, the death rate is 12.5, which is higher than Vietnam (9, 3). On average, five people are killed and 13 seriously injured each day.
