Mondulkiri Provincial Administration Announces Strong Measures to Prevent Forest Crimes and Rejects Some Reports on Large-Scale Timber Smuggling to Neighboring Countries
(Mondulkiri): The territory of Mondulkiri province in northeastern Cambodia is considered a sensitive place for crime and forest smuggling to neighboring countries, especially Vietnam, which borders. In recent days, there have been urgent reports and reports on forest crime and the export of dozens of vehicles to Vietnam by some rich traders and tycoons.
Mr. Sok Sera, Spokesman of Mondulkiri Provincial Hall and Deputy Director of Administration of the Provincial Hall, confirmed that in the recent reports and information on the smuggling of timber to Vietnam, the provincial governor has ordered officials And all relevant law enforcement forces immediately conducted a search at all targets and places with such information, but as a result and finally found that there is no such large-scale crime is not found.
However, according to Mr. Sok Sera, although the results of the investigation have not been seen on a large scale, Governor Svay Sam Eang still ordered all relevant officers and armed forces to continue to monitor. On preventing these forest crimes.
Mr. Keo Sopheak, Director of the Mondulkiri Provincial Department of Environment, confirmed that the image of the advertisement was old and he immediately assigned a professional officer to investigate but could not find it. He said that the above information and reports are very confusing to Mondulkiri province and the management wants to stop all this information.
On the other hand, Mr. Oum Vann Sopheak, Director of Mondulkiri Forestry Administration, was not interested in the reports and information disseminated because there were no large-scale forest crimes. However, he acknowledged that forest crime did occur through the use of motorbikes and cars, but officials continued to crack down.
It is noteworthy that recently, tycoons and big traders who used to trade timber in Cambodia have been suspended after the Royal Government imposed a series of measures and restricted the export of timber.
