(Phnom Penh): It is not only the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, who came out to summon Sam Rainsy to take the oath of office to protect the cleanliness and fairness of the 6th National Assembly election on July 29, 2018 ago, the people who own the vote also came out to protect the ballot to protect their will.
The election process Although many international observers and friendly countries came out to applaud and call it a well-organized election process, for Sam Rainsy, who was nicknamed The cowardly politicians, fearing imprisonment and death, continue to accuse the election of being rigged, unfair, unfair and accused of forcing people to go to the polls.
The baseless allegations made by the fugitive inmate in Cambodia are considered an insult to the will of the electorate as a whole. Voters have come out to confirm and react to such allegations.
Last week, Fresh News interviewed people in Takhmao and Phnom Penh. This week, our unit resumed interviews with people in Kandal Steung district, Kandal province.
Ms. San Sopheap stated, "I know my duty as a citizen. I have to come to the polls to elect members of parliament, especially the leaders of our country. "No one forced me, I came wholeheartedly to vote as my duty as a citizen."
Another older man made the same claim as San Sopheap. Soy Som, 65, who also lives in Chrolong village, Tbeng commune, said that his election on July 29 was a voluntary vote and no one was forcing him to do so.
Mr. Soy Som stated, "No one came to force our own opinion to continue to organize the leaders of our country." "No one forced me."
People in Kandal Steung district, Kandal province, not only expressed confidence in the correctness of the July 29 election process. They also expressed anger at Sam Rainsy, who also accused the election of being rigged.
Ms. San Sopheap reacted to the prisoner Sam Rainsy as follows: "If he says fake is his story. But this election was fair and just at my polling station, there were all national and international observers, all right, there were up to 17 parties in Tbeng commune. "Vote, no one is forcing and no one is violating anyone's rights, everything is right, free and fair."
For Mr. Soy Som, he considered that the remarks made by Sam Rainsy were just a slander of the election results of desperate politicians who had no way to go. But the truth is that the Cambodian people know and see clearly through the large number of people who went to the polls.
Mr. Soy Som stated, "In my opinion, it is difficult to say that people who argue never speak well of each other. It is often said that this is corrupt, that is corrupt, but essentially it depends on the support of the people. "Most people support good ideas, good leadership, see the truth, so ask the international community to recognize and judge, because this is a pure election, no one is forcing anyone."
It should be noted that in order to protect the cleanliness, fairness of the election, as well as the will of more than 83% of the people who went to the polls, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and President of the Cambodian People's Party also came out to call Sam Rainsy. Swear to life, too. However, prisoner Sam Rainsy continued to lie with his cowardice, not daring to come out and swear at the summons of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
The failure to take the oath suggests that Sam Rainsy is afraid of losing his life-threatening oath again because he lost his oath in 2013, endangering the party, causing harm to himself and many members. Prisons fled the country in droves.
In connection with the oath, Khmer civilization expert Rath Sandab said that the oath was not a dead end, but rather increased trust and confidence. He asserted that the oath was like a man running to a court of conscience or a court of law or a moral court to prove his purity or integrity, which is of greater value than a human court.
