Chinese scientists are building a special spacecraft that can help prolong the life of a damaged satellite!
BEIJING (Reuters) - Chinese scientists are developing a new type of spacecraft that could help extend the life of satellites by moving orbiting satellites into orbit. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Monday afternoon, August 13, 2018.
The project is under the research and development of a team of professional engineers of the Chinese Academy of Technology and Science. The new support spacecraft is being designed to be able to connect to satellites that are about to run out of fuel or have technical problems through its robotic arm and help propel the satellite back to the right orbit. The spacecraft can be automated to extend this lifespan.
Research and development for the spacecraft could take up to two years, and once in service, it is expected to extend the operating life of many satellites for at least another 10 years. It takes time to refuel and pull the lost satellite back into orbit.
Spokesperson: NEC decides not to announce official results on 15 August
Mr. Hang Puthea stated, "August 11 is the day to announce the provisional results, and if there is no complaint from that 11th day for 72 hours until Tuesday at 6:59 am, there will be no complaint. The NEC will announce the official results on August 15. ”
"But in the event of a lawsuit, the official announcement of the results will be postponed because it takes time to resolve the complaint," he said. "If there are no witnesses and clear evidence will be rejected ."
The spokesman added that complaints can be made in two places: one at the NEC and two at the Constitutional Council. He added that if the political party that filed the complaint was not satisfied with the NEC decision, the party could continue to file a complaint to the Constitutional Council, but the decision of the Constitutional Council must close the way to protest.
According to Mr. Hang Puthea, according to the law, the NEC must be resolved in a timely and appropriate manner, meaning that it must be completed before September 10, 2018, which is the day the official results are announced.
