(Phnom Penh): What is the link between nationality and the state with the actual situation that an individual has in his family and in the society that creates the rights and duties? The answer is civil status, but nowadays some people are confused as to whether civil status is a birth certificate or just a death certificate.
Pursuant to Sub-Decree No. 103 dated December 29, 2000 on Civil Status, it stipulates that there are 3 types of civil status documents as follows:
If the father / mother of the baby is left for more than 30 days and does not have a birth certificate to use, the applicant or the parent or guardian must apply for the birth certificate (birth certificate) at the relevant commune / sangkat. Real life by bringing with you two witnesses who are adults and must have a real address to confirm in front of the registrar.
In case the family or relative does not report to the registrar within 15 days, the following shall apply: * If it is later than 15 days in the year, the family or relative shall not report to the registrar. Commune / Sangkat Chief of the deceased's residence.
* Cases that are more than one year late and have not yet been reported to the registrar, the family or relatives must appeal to the Capital / Provincial Court (Court) and then take the verdict to the Commune / Sangkat registrar of the deceased for registration. List of registrars. The civil status certificate provides two important benefits to the society and the Cambodian citizens and foreigners living legally in the Kingdom of Cambodia:
* For the society * Help strengthen the rule of law and help build the country: It means that the civil status certificate is a part of law enforcement, because if all citizens have the civil status certificate, then the implementation of the law and the functioning of institutions will take place. Smooth and efficient and effective.
* Strengthen social and national security: means that the civil status certificate confirms the identity of the person with the legal relationship of the country, and the civil status certificate is also an important document for court proceedings.
Failure to register will result in the loss of personal identity. This loss may cause social hardship in relation to the personal interests of the people and failure to fulfill duties to the family as well as society, especially in court proceedings.
Prepared by Mr. Thorn Youseang, Head of Legal Services and Mr. Leang Bunleng, Member of the Working Group for the Preparation and Dissemination of Legal Articles of the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation.
