A review of the situation two weeks ago suggests that China may find it a bit difficult to retaliate against the United States if President Donald Trump does comply with any threat to raise tariffs. China will not retaliate against the United States in the same way that the United States did to China.
And if Beijing were to target US companies operating in China, such as Apple, which could sell more iPhones in China than the United States, or GM, which could sell more products in China than the United States. Since these products are installed locally (China), the Beijing government will be more affected by the economy if the above-mentioned means of retaliation are used.
If the United States raises tariffs on those products, it means the United States will have to pay an additional 25 percent to buy Chinese ingredients for its products. The principle of paying tariffs on imports from China is no different from the United States imposing additional burdens on its industry. Therefore, US companies need to increase domestic production to replace imported products due to the new tax policy.
Just mention the above, it is very serious. But given the current state of the US economy, there is little growth as inflationary pressures begin to mount. As a result, the United States is likely to experience higher inflation and interest rates, while slowing economic growth.
In short, although this situation may make it difficult for China to retaliate against the United States in a trade war, China is still the winner in this condition, as US tariff increases could endanger the US economy itself. More than the impact on China.
