The Chinese government is working to add more cancer drugs to the country's medical insurance coverage. The list already includes most cancer drugs.
According to the requirements of the Chinese National Medical Insurance Administration, the provincial government should work on lowering the prices of medicines included in this list.
The Chinese government is also working to achieve lower prices for cancer patients by adjusting tariffs on these drugs. The 12 pharmaceutical companies participating in the talks are reconsidering the prices of their products.
According to the Medical Insurance Administration, companies are being urged to submit their drug price adjustments to the provincial auction house for the final discount on anti-cancer drugs as soon as possible. .
In addition, the administration has already determined which drugs will be included in the list of drugs for sale after expert selection and voting. Negotiations are expected to be completed by the end of September.
In April, the Chinese Ministry of Finance announced that 28 types of drugs, including all cancer drugs, would be exempt from import duties from May 1.
Launching its business nearly three years ago, this year, Pintor has raised more than $ 1.6 billion by going public. Pinterter combines online shopping with social networking services, with users inviting friends to join a group of shoppers for product discounts. The business venture has helped the company attract more than 300 million users.
Legal expert Leav Yeak said cheaper prices were a major attraction. But a closer look at some of the products reveals that there are some points that the company may have allowed counterfeit products to sell on its website.
"If the shell design is patented, it could be a patent infringement," said Liao Yueq, a legal expert with Beijing Tachi Law Firm. "It is also serious enough to violate anti-competitive laws by tricking customers into believing they are buying a reputable product."
The company said it aims to meet the needs of consumers by providing mid-range products to low-income and elderly buyers, as well as value-conscious customers living in small towns and cities. Rural areas in China.
"When it comes to counterfeit and low quality products, I think the one who is responsible is the seller, the third party," he said. Pintor is not responsible for this. "But the company has a legal obligation to cooperate with regulators in investigating and removing certain products from its website as necessary."
Being listed on the public exchange, the company is under pressure to address these issues. According to Ms. Son.
