Ministry of Water Resources: More than 3,000 hectares of rice in Ba Phnom district, Prey Veng province affected by lack of water, today the Minister ordered the working group to intervene to bring water to farmers
(Prey Veng): A senior official of the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology said that more than 3 hectares of farmers' farmland in Prey Veng province's Ba Phnom district has been affected by drought and lack of irrigation water for growth.
In this regard, the Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology, Mr. Lim Kean Hor, ordered the Secretary of State, Undersecretary of State and the Department of Water Resources of Prey Veng Province, in cooperation with the relevant authorities, to intervene to bring water back to the farmers.
Speaking to Fresh News this morning, August 8, 2018, Mr. Chan Yutha, spokesman for the Ministry of Water Resources, confirmed that so far, the farmers' farmland is only more than 3 hectares in Ba Phnom district, Prey Veng province. And is helping the drought, but the Ministry team led by Ms. Seng Vansay and Undersecretary of State Ms. Van Chantho, in cooperation with the relevant departments and authorities have been intervening.
According to Mr. Chan Yutha, across the country, the working group of the Ministry of Water Resources has intervened to send water to the main river ... so that farmers can bring water to their fields, so the size of The effects of the drought were not serious.
Please note that recently, Cambodia is facing the occurrence of floods that affect four provinces of Cambodia, including: Stung Treng, Kratie, Kampong Cham and Tbong Khmum due to the flood on the Mekong River, causing more than 10,000 families. Many crops and crops were destroyed.
Mr. Chan Yutha added that for Battambang province, in Koas Kralor and Rukkiri districts, the Ministry of Water Resources has been constructing a reservoir called "Dontry" with a capacity of 193 million cubic meters to help supply water. Irrigated hundreds of hectares in that area.
Ek Phnom district, on the other hand, is a low-lying area of the Tonle Sap Lake where people used to cultivate rice to chase water (the water rises when the rice rises), but now the people have turned to dry rice and light rice only when the river floods. Sowing caused flooding.
